@100 = ~Meeting after Dark
Shalvey, a desperate beggar in Brynnlaw, was bitten by a pale, foul-smelling creature near Lady Galvena's establishment. The creature demanded that he bring it victims. He fears a terrible infection and begged me to hunt the monster after nightfall.~
@101 = ~Meeting after Dark
The Ghoul Lord is dead, but Shalvey's bite is still infected. He appears to be turning into a ghoul. I must find a Lesser Restoration or Remove Curse spell or scroll and use it on him before it is too late.~
@102 = ~Meeting after Dark
The Ghoul Lord has been destroyed and Shalvey's ghoul fever has been cured. He thanked us with what little he could offer: his gratitude. The people of Brynnlaw are a little safer.~
@103 = ~Meeting after Dark
The Ghoul Lord was slain, but we refused to cure the infected beggar. Shalvey fled in despair, and our refusal to help may have condemned him to the same fate as his attacker.~

// New English dialogue strings extracted for localisation.
@104 = ~Yes! It worked! My arm is... normal. No pain, no swelling, no odour. I am cured!~
@105 = ~You cured me! You really did it! My arm... it's normal again. I don't know how to repay you. But that creature is still out there. You must kill it, before it preys on someone else.~
@106 = ~Tell me about the creature.~
@107 = ~I will find it.~
@108 = ~Well, it... doesn't itch as much, but... it's still here. And just as infected as ever.~
@109 = ~Thank you again. I can finally sleep without fearing that monster.~
@110 = ~I... I cannot face this nightmare alone.~
@111 = ~Did you find a cure? Please, I have so little time.~
@112 = ~We have a scroll of Lesser Restoration. Let's try it.~
@113 = ~We have a scroll of Remove Curse. Let's try it.~
@114 = ~We'll find a way. Sit tight.~
@115 = ~The creature... is it dead?~
@116 = ~Yes. It was a Ghoul Lord, an undead being driven by cannibalistic hunger. It infected you, and you're turning into one of them.~
@117 = ~Not yet.~
@118 = ~The creature... is it dead?~
@119 = ~Yes. It was a Ghoul Lord. Its sickness could have turned you into one of its kind, but you are safe now.~
@120 = ~Not yet.~
@121 = ~My hand feels so much better now, but that monster is still out there. Were you able to find it?~
@122 = ~Please find him. Behind Galvena's building, after dark... I am so frightened.~
@123 = ~I'll keep looking.~
@124 = ~Could you explain what happened to you again?~
@125 = ~Are... are you still able to heal me?~
@126 = ~Actually, take this potion instead.~
@127 = ~Hold still. I will heal you.~
@128 = ~You're still here... Thank you. What did you want to ask me?~
@129 = ~What happened to your arm? Were you bitten?~
@130 = ~You might need healing.~
@131 = ~We'll talk later.~
@132 = ~P-please... a coin. I need food.~
@133 = ~Living here is difficult. I desperately need help. Just fifty coins would be enough.~
@134 = ~Don't worry. I can spare fifty coins.~
@135 = ~Nalia? What is it?~
@136 = ~I am not a charity shop.~
@137 = ~I... understand. Perhaps someone else will listen.~
@138 = ~(As you pass fifty coins to the beggar and he reaches out his hand, you notice a fresh, infected bite mark. It does not resemble an ordinary animal bite.)~
@139 = ~Thank you. This is more than enough.~
@140 = ~Hold on... what happened to your arm? Were you bitten?~
@141 = ~You might need healing.~
@142 = ~Of course. I'll be going now.~
@143 = ~Be safe, stranger.~
@144 = ~I... it is nothing. Please.~
@145 = ~What happened to you? Who bit you?~
@146 = ~You might need healing.~
@147 = ~Of course. I'll be going now.~
@148 = ~Thank you. I will try to rest.~
@149 = ~Oh, erm... I... I was bitten by an angry dog. A stray d-dog. Nothing more.~
@150 = ~Please... I cannot. He will find me. Just leave.~
@151 = ~Go! Before someone sees us.~
@152 = ~We won't let anyone harm you. We can help, but you must be honest. Whom do you fear?~
@153 = ~I don't want to frighten you. If you want me to leave, I'll leave.~
@154 = ~I... I... (sigh) All right. But this may cost me my life.~
@155 = ~Just tell us. We'll do our best to help.~
@156 = ~My name is Shalvey. I've lived in Brynnlaw for most of my adult life. When Desharik and his cursed pirates took over, I lost everything: my fishing boat, my trade, my home... even my wife. Now I have nowhere to turn, and I sleep outside in the island cold.~
@157 = ~Recently, I was pitching my tent after dark when a hand suddenly grabbed me from behind. I panicked and stabbed at it. The creature didn't even flinch. It stared, then sank its teeth into my hand. I screamed and pushed it into the water, then ran for my life.~
@158 = ~It was pitch-black, and I barely saw anything. Hairless, pale, bald... and it smelled rotten, as though its flesh were decaying. I was too frightened to see more.~
@159 = ~I know what I saw! It wasn't human. I am certain.~
@160 = ~Tell me where you saw this creature. If it's a monster, I'll track it down.~
@161 = ~I have more pressing matters to attend to right now.~
@162 = ~Perhaps that is for the best. I think you should go.~
@163 = ~Wait. There is more. I saw it again the other night. It spoke from the shadows behind Lady Galvena's brothel. It made certain I could not see its face.~
@164 = ~It offered me treasures stolen from old pirate graves if I would lure fresh "meat" to it: drunk men from the docks, unwary travellers wandering near the reef.~
@165 = ~I was so hungry... I almost said yes. But the infection from the bite is festering. I can feel a dark hunger waking up inside my own belly. Please, you must destroy him before I lose my mind and do what he asks! Last I saw him near Galvena's building. He only appears after dark, I think.~
@166 = ~Please, take care out there.~
@167 = ~I... I just need rest. Or perhaps a potion or a spell, if you can spare either.~
@168 = ~Here. Take this Potion of Extra Healing.~
@169 = ~I could cast a healing spell on you.~
@170 = ~I will come back later.~
@171 = ~Thank you. That should make it so much better.~
@172 = ~Well... if you do not mind using your magic on a stranger.~
@173 = ~I'll stay still. Please... be careful.~
@174 = ~I do not know what is happening to me. If I could afford Umberlee's priestesses, perhaps they could help—but I can barely afford bread.~
@175 = ~Thank you for your concern, but... I will be fine.~
@176 = ~I still want to know what happened. Were you bitten?~
@177 = ~Please be careful. I never want anyone else to suffer as I did.~
@178 = ~I... what? Oh, this is bad, this is bad. That explains why my arm is decaying so badly. What can be done? I don't have money for the priestesses' services.~
@179 = ~Please... don't leave me!~
@180 = ~Hold on. We will try to find a way.~
@181 = ~Viconia, can you help him?~
@182 = ~Jaheira, what can be done?~
@183 = ~Cernd, any ideas?~
@184 = ~Aerie, can you lift this curse?~
@185 = ~Anomen, surely you have magic to cure him.~
@186 = ~That is not my problem. I helped you enough.~
@187 = ~Please hurry. Ask at a temple, find a scroll... anything!~
@188 = ~I will be here. Please come back.~
@189 = ~Try it. Try it, please!~
@190 = ~I am ready. Please use the spell on me.~
@191 = ~Hurry! I do not have much time!~
@192 = ~Then please, find it as soon as you can.~
@193 = ~No... n-no! Please.~
@194 = ~I don't know what to do... I'm dead.~
@195 = ~Thank you so much! I really wish I had something to give you. Unfortunately, I haven't got a thing. I promise, however, that I will tell whoever asks that you are decent people with kind hearts.~
@196 = ~What?! That is... horrible! I've never seen such a creature in my life. I've only heard tales. He won't turn anyone anymore, and I am more than happy that you obliterated his undead backside. I have nothing to give you for your help but gratitude. Go with my blessings, and thank you.~
@197 = ~Yyyooouuu... I knoooowww of yooouuu!~
@198 = ~I wiiillll kiiillll yooouuu!~
@199 = ~Hardly. You are nothing more than a ghoul. I eat scum like you for breakfast.~
@200 = ~Why did you attack Shalvey? What is your endgame?~
@201 = ~Iii aaammm noooottt alloooooneeeee!~
@202 = ~Myyy goooaaalll... issss to maaaake thisss place my hoooome. A kiiingdooom of the sea! Yooouuu will pave the waaaay for ussss!~
@203 = ~Must we put up with this bleedin' riff-raff? We're no charity! Go become a fisherman or somethin', ye lousy, stinkin' beggar!~
@204 = ~Shut yer trap, lass! Not all of us have soft hearts to waste on gutter rats. I earned my coin, and I don't hand it to folk too weak to earn their own! Life's a hack-or-be-hacked world, girlie.~
@205 = ~Aye! Feed him, clothe him, find him a job, teach him to wield an axe—and why not make him king of Athkatla while yer at it? Build him a bloody throne!~
@206 = ~Bah! Ye couldn't even fetch us a bleedin' broken copper for all our sweat an' blood, ye miserable, brainless fool!~
@207 = ~But I am not pleased! I joined this group to slaughter a mage, that was the deal. Going back and forth and helping helpless beggars was not part of the agreement, Nalia! I need blood, rush of adrenaline and money!~
@208 = ~Silence, dwarf! Can you not understand that some people lack the privileges you have?~
@209 = ~You are despicable, Korgan. And do not call me 'girlie'. <CHARNAME>, we need to help this man.~
@210 = ~Poor man... dark bags underneath his eyes, pale skin, exhaustion. He looks as though he hasn't eaten or drunk anything in days, if not longer. Maybe we should do more for him.~
@211 = ~Shut it, dwarf. I won't tell you again.~
@212 = ~Here, grab some food. Here are a hundred gold pieces and... huh?~
@213 = ~What is it, Nalia?~
@214 = ~Your arm... you have a massive bite mark on it. Poor thing, what happened to you?~
@215 = ~Oh, you poor man. What did the creature look like? Can you remember any distinguishing features?~
@216 = ~<CHARNAME>, I know we have to rescue Imoen, but we cannot abandon Brynnlaw to a creature that might prey on everyone here. Please reconsider.~
@217 = ~Do not be afraid, Shalvey. We will not let you down.~
@218 = ~Dorn!~
@219 = ~How selfish can you be! I am not leaving this man to suffer that fate! I thought higher of you... well, I suppose this is where we part ways. I am going back to Athkatla. Don't contact me again.~
@220 = ~Korgan, why can't you stop speaking out of turn for once? We committed a good deed. If you had some humanity in you, you'd be pleased with our efforts.~
@221 = ~Why do I waste my breath on you, dwarf? You couldn't see morality if it hit you on the head.~
@222 = ~<CHARNAME>. His hand... did you see it?~
@223 = ~The rot that grips him is like a blight twisting a young tree. We must restore the balance. Lesser Restoration, or magic to remove the curse, should help his spirit renew its strength.~
@224 = ~Those marks suggest he is concealing more than he admits, my friend. We should ask what happened, if only to make certain he is safe.~
@225 = ~Those marks suggest he is concealing more than he admits, my friend. We should ask what happened.~
@226 = ~Those are not a dog's teeth marks. Their size and spacing contradict his story. We must press him, gently if we can.~
@227 = ~Ghoul fever. There are spells capable of reversing it. Lesser Restoration, or Remove Curse, should free him from this infection.~
@228 = ~Nature can overcome many afflictions, but this necrotic poison spreads too quickly. Lesser Restoration or Remove Curse will cleanse his blood before the infection takes his mind.~
@229 = ~This man is clearly exhausted and terrified. Abandoning him would be cruel. We should help him.~
@230 = ~I don't like it. My training taught me that certain undead creatures spread lethal infections. Judging by his wound and blackening veins, I fear he is turning into one of them.~
@231 = ~Perhaps your imagination deceived you. You're old, exhausted, and frail—so frail that the very idea of helping you turns my stomach.~
@232 = ~Such is life.~
@233 = ~Stop yapping, brat! His wellbeing is none of our concern. I don't see why we have to show good-heartedness. We have enough problems to deal with as it is.~
@234 = ~Hmph. Gratitude will hardly fill our purses. Enough of this wretched place.~
@235 = ~Don't. The last thing I need is to be deemed benevolent.~
@236 = ~Rotten stench of death fills my nose whenever I stand close to him. This man is plagued with a sickness, and it isn't a typical flu. It's of a much darker nature.~
@237 = ~Doubtful. That wound is festering quickly. Neither your potion nor simple healing magic will purge what runs through his veins.~
@238 = ~If it were up to me, I would let the fever take him; weaklings deserve no place in the world. But if it silences your incessant whining, rivvil, Lesser Restoration or Remove Curse should purge the filth from his veins.~
@239 = ~We have a term for men who waste our time in the Underdark. It's Jahk. A dimwit, if your brain is still not fathoming it. You fit that definition perfectly for wasting our time like this.~
@240 = ~O-oh, I do know something! The circus elders taught me about the gods' power to heal. Lesser Restoration, or even Remove Curse, should free him from this evil. Please, we must help him before it is too late!~
@241 = ~I can't believe it. You'd leave him? Just like that? He didn't deserve this, and he has no one else to turn to. We practically gave him false hope and betrayed his trust. I... I cannot believe you did this. Please leave me alone for now. I need some time alone.~
@242 = ~Wonderful! My theory was correct! I am so glad it worked out!~
@243 = ~Of course I possess the divine power to purge an unholy affliction! The touch of the undead has corrupted this man's flesh. Lesser Restoration or Remove Curse shall scour the rot from his bones.~
@244 = ~You are leaving him just like that? This world is entangled in cruelty as it is; you need not add more to it. If that is the way you are, I am leaving. May our ways never cross again!~
@245 = ~Torm looks at us with a favourable eye. Undead are a plague to be hunted mercilessly.~
@246 = ~As expected. Another undead horror. It could have turned that man into its servant had we not intervened.~
@247 = ~By Arvoreen! I joined you so we could do some good in this world, not perpetuate its depravity! I hope you will rectify that pathetic choice through your future actions, or else I shall take my leave.~
@248 = ~Pitiful how pirates have made Brynnlaw so lawless that you can't even ask for help when you need it. I dread to think what would have happened if we hadn't shown up. He would have turned into a vicious, cannibalistic ghoul and probably infected other citizens if the pirates hadn't noticed it first. Fortune was on his side.~
@249 = ~I suspect we shall not be paid properly for this. (Why do I bother with these simians?)~
